narrative:
## Building a Complete Calculator

Six operations. Six functions. One `main()` that demonstrates them all.

### Addition, Subtraction, Multiplication, Division

```xiom
fn add(a: Int, b: Int) -> Int {
  return a + b;
}

fn subtract(a: Int, b: Int) -> Int {
  return a - b;
}

fn multiply(a: Int, b: Int) -> Int {
  return a * b;
}

fn divide(a: Int, b: Int) -> Int {
  if b == 0 {
    return 0;
  }
  return a / b;
}
```

### Power (Manual Loop)

Instead of a built-in power operator, build it manually with a loop:

```xiom
fn power(base: Int, exp: Int) -> Int {
  if exp < 0 {
    return 0;
  }
  var result = 1;
  var i = 0;
  while i < exp {
    result = result * base;
    i = i + 1;
  }
  return result;
}
```

### Modulus (Remainder)

```xiom
fn modulus(a: Int, b: Int) -> Int {
  if b == 0 {
    return 0;
  }
  return a % b;
}
```

### Wiring the Calculator

```xiom
fn main() -> Int {
  let x = 12;
  let y = 5;

  let sum = add(x, y);
  let diff = subtract(x, y);
  let prod = multiply(x, y);
  let quot = divide(x, y);
  let pow = power(x, 3);
  let rem = modulus(x, y);

  io.println("Calculator ready!");

  return 0;
}
```

### The Architecture

Each function is a self-contained module. Want to add square roots later? Add `fn sqrt(n: Int) -> Int`. Changing how division handles zero? Edit one function. The modules don't depend on each other — only on their inputs.

